{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nEstablish the provider-neutral `bridge` noun through a safe, working vertical slice. This story is the dependency root for later verb migrations, so it must land without breaking any workflow, environment, script, library caller, or user that still relies on the old vocabulary.\n\nThe earlier plan advertised all eight final verbs before their owning stories implemented them. That would ship broken placeholders on `main`. The corrected staged contract is:\n\n- this story ships only functional `bridge preview` and `bridge sync`;\n- the legacy top-level `reconcile`, `jira-onboard`, `bridge-probe`, and `bridge-fsck` commands remain fully functional;\n- downstream stories add `setup`, `check-access`, `fsck`, `status`, `pause`, and `resume` only together with their real handlers and consumer migrations;\n- no placeholder or not-implemented verb is advertised.\n\nThe final epic surface remains `setup | check-access | fsck | status | preview | sync | pause | resume`. Top-level `rebar doctor` is unrelated store link repair and remains untouched; no `bridge doctor` spelling ships.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- `preview` forces the existing dry-run engine path and performs no Jira or ticket-store mutation.\n- `sync` forces the existing live engine path and mutates.\n- Callers cannot pass `--mode` or `--filter-local-ids` through this foundation and override the read/write distinction.\n- Public `--max-changes`, `--only`, and `--except` remain owned by story `4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51`, which makes preview/sync primary and migrates their consumers in one continuity-preserving change. `--max-age` belongs to `bridge status` and remains owned by status story `b69c-5787-81b8-4052`, whose canonical and legacy status routes share that option.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/mode.py` survives. `MODE_CAPS` and the `Mode` rank ladder remain required by the MCP surface and the phase-gate compatibility value `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"`.\n- Library/MCP parity belongs to `f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d`; workflow/script migration belongs to `4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51`.\n\n## Decomposition\n\nChild `b42a-ffc3-deeb-4491` owns the complete vertical slice: nested dispatch, working preview/sync behavior, help, docs, real CLI oracle, and compatibility coverage. It is intentionally one child rather than an architectural-layer split because the interface must ship with its first consumers.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `rebar bridge preview` is functional and read-only; `rebar bridge sync` is functional and mutating.\n- [x] Group/no-verb/help/unknown-verb behavior is explicit, and nested names never fall through to top-level commands.\n- [x] No sibling-owned verb is advertised before its functional implementation lands.\n- [x] Every legacy command and every current in-repo consumer keeps working at this story boundary.\n- [x] `Mode`, `MODE_CAPS`, and the real `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"` round trip remain intact.\n- [x] Top-level `rebar doctor` remains functional and `bridge doctor` remains unknown.\n- [x] User guide, generated CLI reference, and release notes describe only the staged surface that actually ships.\n- [x] [operator-attested] Child `b42a-ffc3-deeb-4491` passes its held-out behavioral oracle and all project gates.\n\n## Rollback\n\nThe surface is additive and changes no persisted schema. Revert the child's Gerrit change to remove the nested group while leaving all legacy vocabulary and consumers intact."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786221826552904001,"uuid":"8fac7cea-c979-496f-8c9c-769248654417"}